<!--META-DATA
{
	tags: for, sum,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	This program implements the game loop for a player-versus-player (PVE) version of Tic-Tac-Toe.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>state</pf> (1 x 1) number — struct object containing the initial state of a PVE Tic-Tac-Toe game.</p>
			<p><pf>ai_O_level</pf> (1 x 1) number — character indicating which <pf>ai_move</pf> code to use for player O.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>state</pf> (1 x 1) number — struct object containing the final state of a PVE Tic-Tac-Toe game.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>Assume the human player goes first and is the X player. The AI will move as the O player.</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>This should be exactly like your</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>HW5P3_pvp_game_loop</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>code, except that instead of getting a mouse-click selection from the O player, your code will get the selection from one of your ai_move codes based on the value of</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>ai_O_level</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>:</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell>
			<p>
				<ul>
					<li>if <pf>ai_O_level = '1'</pf>, use your <pf>dumb_ai_move</pf> code.</li>
					<li>if <pf>ai_O_level = '2'</pf>, use your <pf>better_ai_move</pf> code.</li>
					<li>if <pf>ai_O_level = '3'</pf>, use your <pf>my_ai_move</pf> code.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

</statement>
